    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d463-a3">

                <pb facs="0896" n="772" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg772"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 217.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morgan to Mr. Mariscal.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-06-28"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 28,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Confidential.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Mariscal</hi>: The day following the one
                    upon which I received your note I telegraphed the Secretary of State that I felt
                    authorized to say that the Mexican Government would oppose no objection to the
                    building of a railroad bridge across the Rio Grande, or near Laredo, by the
                    Mexican Railroad Construction Company, and I asked the secretary whether there
                    would be any objection thereto on the part of the United States.</p>
                <p>Last night I received a telegram from Secretary Blaine in which he informs me
                    that the Executive, while not assuming the right to authorize the construction
                    of a bridge across the national boundary, will permit it to be done, subject to
                    the sanction of Congress and the rights of the State of Texas.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>P. H. MORGAN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
